you've almost certainly heard of mr  beast the 15th largest youtube channel  by subscriber count  made famous for doing extreme stunts  when you see a mr beast thumbnail  your first thought is there's no way  that's real  there's no way he actually gave a random  fan  40 cars there's no way he  actually bought a private island or  planted 20 million  trees if the internet has taught us  anything  it's don't believe what you see today  if you really were the one millionth  visitor you'd never know  and yet jimmy donaldson actually follows  through  it's honest click bait in a way that  brings back some of that early internet  magic  and in december mr beast did it again  this time by opening mr beast burger an  actual restaurant where he handed out  free stacks of cash  apple products and yes even a few  burgers  at the end of the video viewers at home  were told  they too could order a burger from one  of its 300 locations across the country  residents of towns as small as evanston  wyoming  population 11 000 and topsham maine  population 8 000 were surprised to find  they too had a local mr beast burger  location  the app was so popular in fact that it  crashed on that first day two months  later  over a million burgers had been sold  now mr beast had the unique advantage of  not needing to spend a dime on marketing  which might make this whole concept look  like just another influencer brand or  publicity stunt but the most fascinating  thing about mr beastberger  is the fact that it's actually not  unique the way he opened  300 locations in only a matter of months  is the same way tens of thousands of  restaurants have stayed alive during the  pandemic  and a sign of where the restaurant  industry might be headed  sponsored by curiosity stream and nebula  where you can watch the extended version  of this video  no industry has been left untouched by  the pandemic  but some have been forced to adapt more  than others  while airlines were handed over 25  billion dollars in direct grants  small local restaurants were left to  fend for themselves  according to the national restaurant  association 131 billion dollars in sales  were lost in just the first five months  of the pandemic alone  tragic as these circumstances were they  unintentionally created the optimal  conditions for a kind of restaurant  revolution just as residents of new york  city  l.a and the bay area found themselves  paying insane rent with no access to the  city they  loved and decided to take their money  elsewhere restaurant owners  soon came to that same realization they  were still paying for the best real  estate  but who on the other side of a delivery  app would notice if they moved across  town and why stop there if customers  never see  inside the restaurant why pay for  decorations  bathrooms even signs or tables  that's the basic premise behind what are  called cloud  virtual dark or ghost kitchens somewhat  ambiguous terms for restaurants that  don't exist  at least not in the way we typically  think of them  if you've ordered food delivery in the  past few years and if the name of the  restaurant sounded a bit  strange or unfamiliar there's a decent  chance  you've already ordered from one that's  because  they aren't entirely new restaurants and  food trucks have long been able to rent  commissary kitchens as they're known in  the industry to prepare their food  in recent times however and especially  as a response to the pandemic  restaurants have found clever ways of  taking this concept  further one company called reef  technology  for example owns thousands of parking  lots across the country  which of course have mostly sat empty  to make up for lost revenue it placed  delivery-only kitchen trailers in over  90 of its parking lots  with recipes licensed from other  restaurants  kroger the country's largest grocery  chain did something similar by launching  ghost kitchens  inside several of its stores red robin  applebee's chipotle and wendy's to name  just a few  also now operate delivery-only locations  other restaurants stayed put but found  new ways to extract value from their  fixed assets  one of the biggest problems with  restaurants in general  is that for the most part people only  eat during certain times of the day  which means equipment staff and valuable  real estate  sit unused for most of the 24-hour day  but nothing says they have to in the  same way that airbnb  and turo allow individuals to make extra  money from the time they're not using  their home  or car many restaurants now rent out  their unused kitchen time  which is how mr beast pulled off mr  beast burger  your delivery driver will never see a mr  beast sign  instead they might be directed to a  bravo italian kitchen  or hero's grill real restaurants whose  kitchens have been hired by mr beast to  produce his burgers using his  recipes guy fieri also partnered with  existing restaurants to create 130  flavor town kitchens  another way for restaurants to make some  extra cash is by launching their own  ghost kitchens a mexican restaurant for  example  might launch a delivery-only brand of  japanese food  operating out of the same kitchen  without any of its customers knowing the  difference  you can even buy a ghost kitchen  franchise package  for an upfront fee of two thousand  dollars chicago-based wow bow  will send you all the training and  equipment you need to sell its asian  food  out of your restaurant in fact some have  turned this into a feature  since multiple brands are cooked from  the same kitchen they allow you to mix  and match cuisines for the same delivery  fee  so for example you could order some  tacos sushi  and bubble tea all at the same time  usually though  customers have no idea where their food  comes from  last year for instance a woman in  philadelphia ordered pizza from what she  believed to be a locally owned  restaurant  when it arrived it tasted a bit familiar  and not in a good way after doing some  digging she discovered this  local restaurant was actually chuck e  cheese  using a fictional name on grubhub it's  reminiscent of the white label industry  on  amazon where sellers buy repackage  and resell products under different  brand names  for better or worse this is the next  frontier in the  industry the amazonification of food  kitchens are slowly becoming anonymous  warehouses  you don't know or care where your  packages come from  and increasingly neither do we know or  care where our food does  at least that's the hope of companies  like cloud kitchens  the we work for restaurants funded with  a 400 million dollar investment by saudi  arabia's sovereign wealth fund  the company starts by shopping around  for cheap distressed  properties dying bowling alleys auto  repair shops and warehouses  within each of these buildings it  constructs tiny 200 square foot kitchen  cubicles  which chefs can rent on a monthly basis  these shared spaces contain everything a  delivery-only restaurant  needs grills sinks wi-fi electricity  dishwashers and so on some might argue  this takes away all the fun of owning  and operating a restaurant  but there are also advantages think  about it this way  there are good chefs and there are good  business people  some people are both but not all that  means  right now we're missing out on a lot of  good food simply because  some good chefs don't have good business  skills  services like cloud kitchens lower the  barrier to entry  which moves the two circles closer  together  the parallels between cloud kitchens and  silicon valley  are no coincidence the man behind cloud  kitchens  is none other than travis kalanick  former ceo of uber  before he was forced to resign following  a long string of scandals  the question is why restaurants for all  his tech bro  antics and reprehensible behavior no one  doubts kalanick's strategic business  mind  and yet the restaurant industry is  famously  low margin and high risk food  is not software there are few overnight  successes  and there are natural physical limits on  growth  there's a reason in other words none of  the world's largest companies are  involved in food  and why is loud flashy outspoken  kalanick hiding from the spotlight  making sure  cloud kitchens keeps a low media profile  it would seem kalanick is quietly biding  his time  waiting to launch a revolution of the  restaurant industry  cloud kitchens wants to become the  amazon of food  and if anyone can pull it off it's  probably the man behind uber  this is where i explain kalanick's  vision for the future of food  and why he might just be on to something  in the extended version of this video  on nebula just about every other  polymatter video has an extra long  extended version  in part one of china's reckoning for  example i explained why the u.s  is not headed for the same demographic  collapse as  china despite having a similar fertility  rate  nebula is the streaming site i helped  create to give a home to more  experimental  long-form and special videos like  sam from wendover's trivia show joe  scott's mysteries of the human body  and h-bomber guy's breakdown of the true  detective intro  there are no ads videos are available  early and you can watch  entirely exclusive original content  we've partnered with curiositystream  home to great documentaries on  technology  history and science like this one about  why china has one  last remaining steam train so that you  can get it  and nebula for less than 15 bucks a year  sign up with the link in the description  and watch the extended version of this  video  over on nebula

